1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,360
In this video, we'll explain what SQL injection is, describe some common vulnerabilities, explain

2
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:15,840
how to find and exploit SQL injection, and summarize ways to prevent the vulnerability

3
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:16,840
from happening.

4
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:24,960
SQL injection is a web security vulnerability that allows an attacker to interfere with

5
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,980
the queries that an application makes to its database.

6
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:32,260
It generally allows an attacker to view data that they are not normally able to retrieve.

7
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:36,480
This might include data belonging to other users, or any other data that the application

8
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:38,620
itself is able to access.

9
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:43,160
In many cases, an attacker can modify or delete this data, causing persistent changes to the

10
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:45,660
application's content or behavior.

11
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:50,820
In some situations, an attacker can escalate an SQL injection attack to compromise the underlying

12
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:55,380
server or other backend infrastructure, or perform a denial-of-service attack.

13
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:04,260
Consider a shopping application that displays products in different categories.

14
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:10,020
When the user clicks on the GIFs category, their browser requests this URL.

15
00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:14,660
This causes the application to make an SQL query to retrieve details of the relevant products

16
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:15,660
from the database.

17
00:01:15,660 --> 00:01:24,940
This SQL query asks the database to return all details from the products table, where the

18
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:30,140
category is GIFs, and released is 1.

19
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:35,020
The restriction that released is 1 is being used to hide products that are not released.

20
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:38,320
For unreleased products, presumably released is 0.

21
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:43,320
The application doesn't implement any defenses against SQL injection attacks, so an attacker

22
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:51,560
can construct an attack like this, which results in this SQL query.

23
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:56,620
The key thing here is that the double dash sequence is a comment indicator in SQL, and means that

24
00:01:56,620 --> 00:01:59,800
the rest of the query is interpreted as a comment.

25
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:04,320
This effectively removes the remainder of the query, meaning that all products are displayed,

26
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:06,320
including unreleased products.

27
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:11,320
Going further, the attacker can cause the application to display all the products in any category,

28
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:14,180
including categories they don't know about.

29
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:21,500
To do this, the attacker can send this attack, which results in this SQL query.

30
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:27,320
The modified query will return all items where either the category is GIFs, or 1 is equal

31
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:28,740
to 1.

32
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:35,740
Since 1 equals 1 is always true, the query will return all items.

33
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:40,900
Consider an application that lets users log in with a username and password.

34
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:45,420
If a user submits the username wiener and the password blue cheese, the application checks

35
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:49,020
the credentials by performing this SQL query.

36
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:53,220
If the query returns the details of a user, then the login is successful.

37
00:02:53,220 --> 00:02:55,340
If not, then the login is rejected.

38
00:02:55,340 --> 00:03:00,100
In this situation, an attacker can log in as any user without a password simply by using

39
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:05,060
the SQL comment sequence to remove the password check from the WHERE clause of the query.

40
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:10,260
If the attacker submits the username administrator quote dash dash, then the application performs

41
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:12,980
this SQL query.

42
00:03:12,980 --> 00:03:18,540
This query returns the user whose username is administrator, and successfully logs the attacker

43
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:23,300
in as that user.

44
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:28,040
In cases where the results of an SQL query are returned within the application's responses,

45
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:34,260
an attacker can leverage an SQL injection vulnerability to retrieve data from other tables within the database.

46
00:03:34,260 --> 00:03:39,300
This is done using the union keyword, which lets you execute an additional select query,

47
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:42,660
and append the results to the original query.

48
00:03:42,660 --> 00:03:48,940
If an application executes this query containing the user input gifs, then an attacker can submit

49
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:50,880
this input.

50
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:54,880
This will cause the application to return all usernames and passwords, along with the names

51
00:03:54,880 --> 00:04:00,260
and descriptions of products.

52
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:04,640
When you've discovered an SQL injection vulnerability, it's generally useful to obtain some information

53
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:06,320
about the database itself.

54
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:10,140
This information can often pave the way for further exploitation.

55
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:13,080
You can query the version details for the database.

56
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:17,700
The way that this is done depends on the database type, so you can infer the database type from whichever

57
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:19,160
technique works.

58
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:24,820
For example, on Oracle, you can execute this query to show the database version.

59
00:04:24,820 --> 00:04:29,640
You can also determine what database tables exist, and which columns they contain.

60
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:38,180
For example, on most databases, you can execute this query to list the tables in the database.

61
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:41,660
Many instances of SQL injections are blind vulnerabilities.

62
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:45,680
This means that the application does not return the results of the SQL query or the details of

63
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:48,840
any database errors within its responses.

64
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:53,140
Blind vulnerabilities can still be exploited to access unauthorized data, but the techniques

65
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:57,120
involved are generally more complicated and difficult to perform.

66
00:04:57,120 --> 00:05:00,860
Depending on the nature of the vulnerability and the database involved, a variety of techniques

67
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:05,060
can be used to exploit blind SQL injection vulnerabilities.

68
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:09,620
You can change the logic of the query to trigger a detectable difference in the application's response,

69
00:05:09,620 --> 00:05:12,160
depending on the truth of a single condition.

70
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:17,080
This might involve injecting a new condition into some Boolean logic, or conditionally triggering

71
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:19,720
an error such as a divide by zero.

72
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:23,620
You can conditionally trigger a time delay in the processing of the query, allowing you to

73
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:28,620
infer the truth of the condition based on the time that the application takes to respond.

74
00:05:28,620 --> 00:05:31,620
You can also trigger an out-of-band network interaction.

75
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:36,160
This technique is extremely powerful and works in situations where the other techniques do not.

76
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:41,380
Often, you can directly exfiltrate data via the out-of-band channel, for example, by placing

77
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:48,540
the data into a DNS lookup for a domain that you control.

78
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:52,980
The majority of SQL injection vulnerabilities can be found quickly and reliably using Burp

79
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:55,420
Suite's Web Vulnerability Scanner.

80
00:05:55,420 --> 00:06:00,060
You can find SQL injection vulnerabilities manually by using a systematic set of tests against

81
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:02,240
every entry point in the application.

82
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:06,560
You can submit the single quote character and look for errors or other anomalies.

83
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:11,540
You can submit some SQL specific syntax and look for systematic differences in the resulting

84
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:13,680
application responses.

85
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:18,920
You can submit Boolean conditions and look for differences in the application's responses.

86
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:22,280
You can submit payloads designed to trigger time delays and look for differences in the

87
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:24,700
time taken to respond.

88
00:06:24,700 --> 00:06:29,060
And you can submit payloads designed to trigger an out-of-band network interaction and monitor

89
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:35,640
for any resulting interactions.

90
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:40,580
Most SQL injection vulnerabilities arise within the WHERE clause of a SELECT query.

91
00:06:40,580 --> 00:06:45,800
This type of SQL injection is generally well understood by experienced testers, but SQL injection

92
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:50,280
vulnerabilities can in principle occur at any location within the query and within different

93
00:06:50,280 --> 00:06:52,000
query types.

94
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:57,740
The most common other locations where SQL injection arises are in UPDATE statements within the UPDATED

95
00:06:57,740 --> 00:07:01,080
VALUES or the WHERE clause.

96
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:06,760
In INSERT statements within the INSERTED VALUES, in SELECT statements within the TABLE or COLUMN

97
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:15,320
NAME, and in SELECT statements within the ORDER BY clause.

98
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:21,300
First order SQL injection arises where the application takes the user input from an HTTP request and,

99
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:27,580
in the course of processing that request, incorporates the input into an SQL query in an unsafe way.

100
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:33,120
In second order SQL injection, also known as stored SQL injection, the application takes

101
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:37,300
user input from an HTTP request and stores it for future use.

102
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:41,640
This is usually done by placing the input into a database, but no vulnerability arises at the

103
00:07:41,640 --> 00:07:43,520
point where the data is stored.

104
00:07:43,520 --> 00:07:48,880
Later, when handling a different HTTP request, the application retrieves the stored data and

105
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:52,900
incorporates it into an SQL query in an unsafe way.

106
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:57,280
The same order SQL injection often arises in situations where developers are aware of SQL

107
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:01,540
injection vulnerabilities and so safely handle the initial placement of the input into the

108
00:08:01,540 --> 00:08:02,860
database.

109
00:08:02,860 --> 00:08:07,020
When the data is later processed, it is deemed to be safe, since it was previously placed

110
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:08,920
into the database safely.

111
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:13,260
At this point, the data is handled in an unsafe way, because the developer wrongly deems

112
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:17,900
it to be trusted.

113
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:24,160
Some core features of the SQL language are implemented in the same way across popular database platforms,

114
00:08:24,160 --> 00:08:28,640
and so many ways of detecting and exploiting SQL injection vulnerabilities work identically

115
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:30,360
on different types of database.

116
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:34,540
However, there are also many differences between common databases.

117
00:08:34,540 --> 00:08:38,660
These mean that some techniques for detecting and exploiting SQL injection work differently

118
00:08:38,660 --> 00:08:41,480
on different platforms.

119
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:47,860
The difference between databases arise in areas like syntax for string concatenation, comments,

120
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:58,800
batched or stacked queries, platform-specific APIs, and error messages.

121
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:03,420
Most instances of SQL injection can be prevented by using parameterized queries instead of string

122
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:06,000
concatenation within the query.

123
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:11,600
This code is vulnerable to SQL injection because the user input is concatenated directly into

124
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:12,980
the query.

125
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:16,940
The code can be easily rewritten in a way that prevents the user input from interfering with

126
00:09:16,940 --> 00:09:19,660
the query structure.

127
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:24,580
Parameterized queries can be used for any situation where untrusted input appears as data within

128
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:30,300
the query, including the WHERE clause and values in an insert or update statement.

129
00:09:30,300 --> 00:09:34,480
They can't be used to handle untrusted input in other parts of the query, such as table

130
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:38,020
or column names, or the ORDER BY clause.

131
00:09:38,020 --> 00:09:41,820
Application functionality that places untrusted data into those parts of the query will need

132
00:09:41,820 --> 00:09:46,480
to take a different approach, such as whitelisting permitted input values, or using different

133
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:50,000
logic to deliver the required behavior.

134
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,200
For a parameterized query to be effective in preventing SQL injection, the string that is

135
00:09:54,200 --> 00:09:59,360
used in the query must always be a hard-coded constant, and must never contain any variable

136
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:01,400
data from any origin.

137
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:04,940
Don't be tempted to decide case by case whether an item of data is trusted.

138
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:09,220
It is all too easy to make mistakes about the possible origin of data, or for changes

139
00:10:09,220 --> 00:10:12,480
in other code to violate assumptions about what data is tainted.

